<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-09-23.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="002911"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002911" label="[00:29:11]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;cosinusoidally&gt; Fun fact, the latest version of mawk (mawk-1.3.4-20240905.tgz) builds out of the box on a slackware 2.0.0 system from 1994. Even the configure script works. It also ships 2 pregenerated files (parse.c and parse.h) that were generated by byacc. I was able to use this version of mawk to run my awk versions of a subset of m2/m0/hex2.</span></div><div class="line" id="003146"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#003146" label="[00:31:46]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;cosinusoidally&gt; I was having some issues with gawk, but it turns out I just needed to export LC_ALL=C</span></div><div class="line" id="055131"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#055131" label="[05:51:31]">&lt;fosslinux&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that is rather a surprise, cosinusoidally!</span></div><div class="line" id="055748"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#055748" label="[05:57:48]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nimaje: Thanks for the comments, I'm not sure if it's meant to be efficient, I was trying to go for clarity</span></div><div class="line" id="060956"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#060956" label="[06:09:56]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">What I'm interested in is how lexer generators work, I'm well aware that there are plenty of ways do it it with regexps in fewer LOC.</span></div><div class="line" id="082408"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#082408" label="[08:24:08]">&lt;nimaje&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I would assume a lexer generator would build something to only hold as much state as needed and not building lists or long strings everywhere</span></div><div class="line" id="224352"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224352" label="[22:43:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: I want to add a couple of amd64 M1 definitions to M2libc <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/M2libc/pull/60">https://github.com/oriansj/M2libc/pull/60</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="224439"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224439" label="[22:44:39]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this is for reading and writing to CR3 register (which of course only works in ring0)</span></div><br /></div></body></html>